Thanks y’all (as well as those who chimed in on twitter <https://twitter.com/noamross/status/628335418686222336>). After a few minutes of trying to read the manual for awk, I decided to follow my own advice <http://www.noamross.net/blog/2013/4/25/faster-talk.html> and just find a machine that could hold the file in memory.

In this case, a combination of floor() and duplicated() makes short work of the problem:
library(readr)
library(dplyr)
 #readr's read_delim is nice and fast way to lead files:
output = read_delim("micro.txt", " ", col_names = FALSE)
colnames(output) = c("run", "start", "time", "control", as.character(0:(ncol(output)-5)))
micro_output_short = output %>%
  group_by(run) %>%
  # Here, I round all the times down to the nearest integer, and pick the rows where
  # the integer is different than the last, working up from the bottom.
  filter((!duplicated(floor(time), fromLast=TRUE)) | time == 0) %>% #
  group_by() %>%
  # Then I convert the times to the integer number
  mutate(time = ceiling(time))
​

