​Seems you already solved this, but here are my 2 cents, maybe useful if you find yourself without access to a machine with enough memory for R in the future.

I think this awk one-liner will do the trick
​
:

awk '{if ($3 == 0) {
​​
​​
print prev; print $0; prev=$0; prevint=int($3)} else if (int($3) != prevint) {print prev; prev=$0; prevint=int($3)} else  prev=$0; prevint=int($3)}'
​​
input.txt
​ > output.txt​

It wasn't clear
​ to me​
 if you wanted to print out the last row before the counter resets to 0, this will do that though.
​ If that is not what you want, just remove ​
​
​​
print prev;
​ from ​the first block. I think I took all the edge cases into account here, but it's possible I missed some since I don't have your actual dataset.

​If you end up running it I would be interested if you got the same result as you did with R​

Best,
Tyson

