also with the caviat that take this with a grain of salt given you already have a solution.  But another option would be to iterate through the array/dataframe in reverse and grab any data point that has a new int/floor/trunc. I'm not sure what the performance difference would be with comparisons to integers vs floats, but I would guess that ints would be slightly faster to process and compare; so if the "prev" variable is just the integer component of the previous value.

I'm not sure how easily R handles array indexing in reverse, in particular if it uses non-contiguous blocks in memory for flexible length arrays, so your performance may take a hit with this method vs the already suggested ones. But if it handles forward and backward iteration equally, this might be a bit faster than forward indexing within R.

